A slot's invocation list may carry a component-typed entry, and the insertion
populates it by name -- the way a call site populates a component's slot. It is
how the directory importer hands a page's blocks to a layout the program
supplies once.

The insertion's block keeps its meaning: bare nodes there are the slot's
fallback. So `layout(fm) { component content { … } }` is the page and
`layout(fm) { … }` is what renders when nothing populates `layout`.

The content is written in `site`'s body and reads `site`'s `pages`, which is
renamed per instance when `site` is inlined. The population binds the entry
positionally under a name of its own, `body`, and inserts it like any slot,
fallback included -- the second page supplies no content and renders
`no content supplied`. It inserts it inside `frame`'s own population, which is
where a layout puts its content; the inliner expands `frame` there before
`site` is spliced, so the entry is a direct insertion by the time it is
substituted.

deny * `fallback for an unpopulated layout` -- layout is populated, so the insertion's fallback is not rendered

-- m.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

struct Frontmatter {
    title string = ""
}

component frame(main component node) node {
    vbox {
        main
    }
}

component site(layout list<component(fm Frontmatter, content component node) node>) node {
    var pages = 2

    vbox {
        layout(Frontmatter{title="One"}) {
            component content {
                text(value="first of {pages} pages")
            }
        }
        layout(Frontmatter{title="Two"}) {
            text(value="fallback for an unpopulated layout")
        }
    }
}

window #w {
    site {
        component layout(fm, body) {
            vbox {
                text(value=fm.title)
                frame {
                    component main {
                        body {
                            text(value="no content supplied")
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
